Built-in Functional Interfaces

Interface	        Purpose         	Method
Predicate<T>	Tests a condition	    test()
Function<T,R>	Transforms a value	    apply()
Consumer<T>	    Consumes a value	    accept()
Supplier<T>	    Supplies a value	    get()

1. Predicate - A Predicate is a functional interface that takes an input and returns a boolean (true or false).

            import java.util.function.Predicate;

            public class Main {
                public static void main(String[] args) {

                    Predicate<Integer> isEven = n -> n % 2 == 0;

                    System.out.println(isEven.test(10));  // abstract method is test()
                    System.out.println(isEven.test(7));
                }
            }

2. Function - A Function takes an input, performs some operation, and returns an output. It can return any type.

            import java.util.function.Function;

            public class Main {
                public static void main(String[] args) {

                    Function<Integer, Integer> multiply = n -> n * 2;

                    System.out.println(multiply.apply(5));  // abstract method is apply()
                }
            }

3. Consumer - A Consumer takes an input and performs some operation on it, but does not return any output.

            import java.util.function.Consumer;

            public class Main {
                public static void main(String[] args) {

                    Consumer<String> print = s -> System.out.println(s);

                    print.accept("Hello, World!");  // abstract method is accept()
                }
            }

    - forEach() is a method that accepts a Consumer as an argument and applies an operation to each collection element

    - andThen() In Java 8, Consumer provides a default method To execute two Consumer operations one after another on the same input.

        Consumer<String> c1 = name -> System.out.println("Hello " + name);
        Consumer<String> c2 = name -> System.out.println("Welcome " + name);

        c1.andThen(c2).accept("Java");

    andThen() executes the second Consumer only after the first completes successfully. If the first throws an exception, the second won't execute

        Method	        Functional interface
        List.forEach()	    Consumer
        Map.forEach()	    BiConsumer  ~ BiConsumer<T, U> → accepts two inputs.

4. Supplier - A Supplier takes no input but returns a value. Example: Generate a random number, Return a username.

            import java.util.function.Supplier;

            public class Main {
                public static void main(String[] args) {

                    Supplier<Double> randomValue = () -> Math.random();

                    System.out.println(randomValue.get());  // abstract method is get()
                }
            }

UnaryOperator vs BinaryOperator
 Both are special cases of Function interface. They are used when the input and output types are the same.

    Interface	             Purpose
    ---------------------------------
    Function	        One input, potentially different output type
    UnaryOperator	    One input, same output type
    BiFunction	        Two inputs, potentially different output type
    BinaryOperator      Two inputs, same output type